  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nImagine a side-drawer that is implemented as a `<dialog>`, but then you also want a global toast that can show on-top of the dialog backdrop (and dialog). Right now. because top layer ordering is controlled by opening order, this means that one would have to make the toast a `popover` and then close and reopen it every time something else gets added to the top-layer, so that the toast is always at the top of the top layer.\n\nThis has two problems: there is no reliable way to know when something is added to the top layer AFAICT, and also this just seems incredibly hacky :D\n\nIs there a better way? Do we need a z-index for top-layer?\n\nThe fact that there is now `overlay: none` to just disable all top-layer handling for popovers and dialogs suggests that maybe it is not the right abstraction. Should we reconsider the entirety of the top layer?",
